title: Sigma and YARA, hands-on
guide: howto/sigma-and-yara.html
promise: Write a Sigma rule for log events and convert it for Splunk, then a YARA rule for file bytes, tested on a harmless file too.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is intermediate: you have used a SIEM (security information and event management system) or a log search language, use a terminal and read YAML, the indented name: value format of Sigma rules.

Allow two hours; you need no SIEM.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
Sigma rules and commands ran as printed with sigma-cli 4.0.0, YARA ones with yara-x-cli 1.23.0, on an Apple silicon Mac; outputs shown are what they printed.

Not re-run: step 6's install. Step 5's correlation rule was converted for Splunk only.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Paste BASH blocks into your terminal; for a demo.yar tab, paste step 7's whole block, EOF line included. YAML blocks are a Sigma rule or part of one. YARA blocks are rules to read and try. Unlabelled blocks are output.

== Set up Sigma: sigma-cli and the Splunk backend
section: 1. Install the toolchain
sigma-cli checks and converts Sigma rules. It needs Python 3.11 to 3.14 (swap python3.13 for yours) and installs into .venv, a virtual environment. Then install the Splunk backend: sigma plugin install splunk. Versions other than sigma-cli 4.0.0 may print differently.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/detect-lab && cd ~/detect-lab
python3.13 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python3 -m pip install sigma-cli
sigma version
```

== Set up YARA: YARA-X, not YARA
section: 6. Install YARA-X, not YARA
YARA-X is a rewrite of YARA, whose 4.x line gets bug fixes only. Its command is yr, not yara. You can swap the first line for cargo install yara-x-cli. The guide used yara-x-cli 1.23.0 but did not re-run this install.
```bash
brew install yara-x
yr --version
```

== Two rule formats for two different jobs
section: Two rule formats, two completely different jobs
In a cloud incident, Sigma answers what happened, from log events, and YARA answers what a file the intruder left is, from its bytes. A Sigma rule is converted into your SIEM's query language; YARA runs its rules itself.

== Sigma step 2: a rule's detection
section: 2. The anatomy of a rule
Step 2 saves a rule for AccessDenied errors in CloudTrail, the record of calls made in an Amazon Web Services account, as aws_cloudtrail_access_denied_burst.yml. In this part, the condition needs the selection block to match and filter_known_scanner not to.
```yaml
detection:
  # Each named block is a "search identifier". Keys ANDed, list values ORed.
  selection:
    errorCode: AccessDenied
  filter_known_scanner:
    userIdentity.arn|startswith: 'arn:aws:sts::111122223333:assumed-role/config-recorder/'
  condition: selection and not filter_known_scanner
```

== Sigma step 3: validate before you convert
section: 3. Validate before you convert
Expect Found 0 errors, 0 condition errors and 0 issues. Under a short name such as rule.yml, the file gets a high-severity FilenameLengthIssue. By default the sigma check command still exits 0, so in CI (continuous integration) add --fail-on-issues.
```bash
sigma check aws_cloudtrail_access_denied_burst.yml
```

== The one idea: the field names are Sigma's
section: 4. Convert it, and meet the pipeline requirement
This fails on purpose, asking for a processing pipeline. A Sigma rule's field names are Sigma's, not your SIEM's; a pipeline maps them onto your data's. Without one, the query runs, returns zero results and looks like a quiet environment.
```bash
sigma convert -t splunk aws_cloudtrail_access_denied_burst.yml
```

== Sigma step 4: convert without a pipeline
section: 4. Convert it, and meet the pipeline requirement
Add --without-pipeline, knowing the cost, and it prints this Splunk search: |startswith became a trailing *, and the not keyword became NOT. The -t flag picks the SIEM, but not every backend works with sigma-cli 4.0.0 yet.
```text
errorCode="AccessDenied" NOT userIdentity.arn="arn:aws:sts::111122223333:assumed-role/config-recorder/*"
```

== Sigma step 5: correlation counts a burst
section: 5. Correlation rules
Step 2's rule fires on a single denied event. Step 5 appends a second YAML document to the same file, a correlation rule counting matches over a window. Above this part, copy the guide's ---, title, id and status.
```yaml
correlation:
  type: event_count
  rules:
    - aws_cloudtrail_access_denied          # the `name:` of the base rule
  group-by:
    - sourceIPAddress
  timespan: 10m
  condition:
    gte: 10
level: high
```

== YARA step 7: one string from each group
section: 7. Write a rule, and a target to test it on
Step 7 saves demo.yar, a rule for a script that reads cloud credential files and sends them off. Above this part, $cred1, $cred2 and $imds match two credential paths and an address.
```yara
        // fullword = must be delimited, so "curl" does not match "curling"
        $exfil1 = "curl" fullword ascii nocase
        $exfil2 = "wget" fullword ascii nocase

        // A regular expression string. RE2-style: no backreferences.
        $exfil3 = /https?:\/\/[a-z0-9.-]+\/(upload|collect|p)\b/ nocase

    condition:
        filesize < 512KB
        and any of ($cred*, $imds)
        and any of ($exfil*)
```

== YARA step 8: the harmless file must not match
section: 8. Compile, scan, and read the evidence
Step 7's last lines write sample.sh, which must match, and benign.txt, which contains curl but must not. Expect Suspicious_Cloud_Credential_Theft sample.sh, then nothing. That negative case alone tells a working rule from one that matches half your filesystem.
```bash
yr scan demo.yar sample.sh
yr scan demo.yar benign.txt
```

== YARA step 8: read the evidence
section: 8. Compile, scan, and read the evidence
The -s flag, as in yr scan -s demo.yar sample.sh, prints each matched string's offset, length, identifier and bytes. This is how you debug a rule and write up a finding: a colleague can verify a match with offsets.
```text
Suspicious_Cloud_Credential_Theft sample.sh
0xf:17:$cred1: /.aws/credentials
0x2d:4:$exfil1: curl
0x3a:25:$exfil3: https://evil.test/collect
```

== YARA step 10: keep rules in git like code
section: 10. Keep rules in git like code
The yr fmt command rewrites a rule file in canonical style, exiting 1 when it does. With --check it changes nothing but exits 1 if it would: a CI gate. Here, silently, the first rewrites demo.yar; the second exits 0.
```bash
yr fmt demo.yar
yr fmt --check demo.yar
```

== Exercise: a Sigma query that finds nothing
section: Hands-on exercises
Your converted Splunk query runs against a year of CloudTrail and returns zero results. Give three distinct explanations, most likely first, and say how you would tell them apart. The guide hides each answer until you open Show the answer.

== Answer: three explanations, most likely first
section: Hands-on exercises
- With no pipeline, the field names are wrong. Search for one field alone.
- The data is not in the index you searched. Search it for any CloudTrail event.
- Least likely, nothing happened. Conclude that only after ruling out both.

== Exercise: a YARA rule matching every script
section: Hands-on exercises
This YARA rule was written to catch a credential stealer. It matches roughly every shell script on the system. Fix it, and explain the class of mistake.
```yara
rule Credential_Stealer
{
    strings:
        $a = "aws"
        $b = "curl"
        $c = "cat"
    condition:
        any of them
}
```

== Answer: indicators for behaviour, not tools
section: Hands-on exercises
Three failures compound: short common strings, a condition met by any one of them, and no fullword, so $a matches inside laws.
```yara
rule Credential_Stealer
{
    strings:
        $path  = "/.aws/credentials" ascii nocase
        $imds  = "169.254.169.254"   ascii
        $send1 = "curl" fullword ascii nocase
        $send2 = "wget" fullword ascii nocase
    condition:
        filesize < 512KB
        and any of ($path, $imds)      // a credential source, AND
        and any of ($send*)            // a way to send it off the box
}
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Without the falsepositives and level fields, analysts cannot triage alerts.
- Never reuse or omit a Sigma rule's id, which survives renames.
- Without the ascii wide modifiers, a YARA string misses its Windows-binary form.
- Test both formats on harmless data too.
